{"n": 1, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Definition 2.1(2) introduces the term: \"Dually, we say that a morphism e_1 → e_2 in ℰ is f-cartesian (or simply cartesian) if it induces a pullback diagram\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 2, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "Example 1.6 says \"The forgetful functor Top → Set is a cartesian fibration\", which asserts a property of an object rather than a specialisation of one result or construction from another, so the rubric's has-property applies.", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 3, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Remark 2.3 says \"A cocartesian fibration ℰ → ℬ whose fibers f^{-1}(b) are all ∞-groupoids is called a left fibration\", so a left fibration is a cocartesian fibration with an added hypothesis.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The same passage also gives the inclusion \"ℒℱib(ℬ) ⊂ coCℱib(ℬ)\", and it is primarily a definition. The rubric does not clearly decide whether adding a hypothesis to a notion counts as generalises, subobject-of, or only defines."}
{"n": 4, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Theorem 3.3 states: \"Suppose that a map Δ^1 → E in sSet_Joyal presents a p-cartesian morphism [1] → ℰ. Then the edge f ∈ E_1 is JL-p-cartesian.\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 5, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Remark 1.3 introduces the term: \"a morphism in C^⊗ is called p-inert (or simply inert) if it is p-cocartesian and lies over an inert morphism in Fin_*\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 6, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Definition 2.1(1) says: \"We say that a morphism e_1 → e_2 in ℰ is f-cocartesian (or simply cocartesian ...) if it induces a pullback diagram\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 7, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "Example 1.6 says \"The forgetful functor Top → Set is a cartesian fibration\", which is a property of an object (has-property), not specialisation.", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 8, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "Example 1.5 says \"the forgetful functor VectBdl → Mfld to the category of manifolds is a cartesian fibration\", which is a property of an object (has-property), not specialisation.", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 9, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Example 1.2 says \"a symmetric monoidal functor ... is equivalent data to that of a morphism of cocartesian fibrations ... over Fin_*\" and \"the fact that the functor respects the monoidal products is encoded by the fact that it preserves cocartesian morphisms\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 10, "grade": "wrong-modality", "reason": "Remark 1.4 says \"The observations of 1.2 and 1.3 form the foundations of the extremely versatile theory of ∞-operads introduced and studied in [Lur14, Chapter 2]\". That the observations are foundational is this paper's own assertion; only the theory itself is attributed to Lur14.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "It is unclear whether the modality should follow the cited theory (Lur14) or the paper's own claim that the observations are foundational. It is also unclear whether \"form the foundations\" meets depends-on's \"invoked as a step\" test, although the rubric's Nöbeling example suggests it does."}
{"n": 11, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Theorem 3.3 says that if f \"presents a p-cartesian morphism [1] → ℰ. Then the edge f ∈ E_1 is JL-p-cartesian\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 12, "grade": "wrong-relation", "reason": "Example 1.2 says \"the identity map of Fin_* is a cocartesian fibration\", which is a property of an object (has-property), not specialisation.", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 13, "grade": "not-in-paper", "reason": "The input gives only the statement of Corollary 3.4 (\"If p is a cartesian fibration, then p is a JL-cartesian fibration\") and no sentence that invokes Theorem 3.3 as a step.", "doubt": true, "doubt_reason": "The proof of Corollary 3.4 is not in the input. Its label as a corollary placed right after Theorem 3.3 suggests the dependency, but the paper text given does not quote it."}
{"n": 14, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Example 1.2 says \"It follows that we can equivalently consider a symmetric monoidal category (C,⊗) as a cocartesian fibration C^⊗ → Fin_* over the category of finite pointed sets\".", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
{"n": 15, "grade": "faithful", "reason": "Definition 2.1(2) says: \"We then say that the functor f is a cartesian fibration if every object of Fun([1],ℬ) ×_{t,ℬ,f} ℰ admits an f-cartesian lift.\"", "doubt": false, "doubt_reason": ""}
