{"p4": {"friction": "Five claims (2, 7, 8, 12, 18) use `specialises` for 'X is a cartesian/cocartesian fibration' (e.g. \"The forgetful functor Top → Set is a cartesian fibration\"). The rubric covers specialisation of results but has no near-miss for 'an object is an instance of a defined notion'. Remark 2.3's \"is called a left fibration\" sits on three boundaries at once: defines, generalises and subobject-of.", "doubt": "Claim 10: modality and depends-on admissibility for \"form the foundations of ... [Lur14]\". Claim 13: no proof text for Corollary 3.4. Claim 19: whether \"Following [Lur09, Definition 2.4.1.1]\" makes the modality cited. Claim 3: whether adding a hypothesis to a notion counts as generalises.", "missing_context": "The proofs in section 3 (needed for claim 13's depends-on), and a rule on modality when a paper restates a cited definition.", "proposed_change": "Add a near-miss to `generalises`: 'X is a [defined notion]' (an object is an instance of a class) is `has-property`, not `specialises`. Also state whether a notion defined by adding a hypothesis to another notion (left fibration ⊂ cocartesian fibration) is `generalises` or `subobject-of`.", "process_observation": "The extractor produced duplicate claims (2/7 and 8/18 are near-identical; 4/11 restate the same theorem), which inflates the counts. The paper text is LaTeXML-mangled, with diagrams flattened into token streams, which makes locator checks for diagram-defined content harder."}}
